nd tender/swollen areas.":["u355"],"copy|Specific circumstances: genital trauma/infection suggesting sexual abuse, concerning fractures, and an unusual delay in seeking help (p214).":["u356"],"copy|Distinguish the screening instruments.":["u357"],"copy|The same AMT4 orientation questions appear within 4AT delirium screening, ED dementia screening, and Senior Aid abuse screening; this chapter does not place AMT4 in an inpatient depression/suicide screen (Figures 15-2/15-3/15-4, pp211–214).":["u358"],"copy|Fall interventions show mixed results: ED medication deprescribing alone did not reduce falls at 1 year, whereas a brief individualized PT/pharmacy intervention reduced overall and fall-related returns at 6 months.":["u359"],"copy|Local champions, coordination and accessible follow-up matter.":["u360"],"copy|Existing vulnerability instruments do not accurately identify both high- and low-risk subsets for the broad short-term outcomes under discussion (p214).":["u361"],"copy|Figure 15-4 — complete Senior Aid Tool and assessment items":["u362"],"copy|Function after discharge.":["u255"],"copy|Establish earlier ability to transfer, dress, take medicines and obtain food.":["u256"],"copy|Then ask what changed with the acute episode and who can help tonight.":["u257"],"copy|Someone already receiving paid help may still lose additional function.":["u258"],"copy|Someone living alone may have reliable nearby support.":["u259"],"copy|The chapter compares ED vulnerability tools against several distinct outcomes, including return visits, institutionalization and functional loss.":["u260"],"copy|Most do not separate high- and low-risk groups well enough to settle disposition.":["u261"],"copy|The Clinical Frailty Scale shows more promise for 30-day mortality than for predicting preventable loss of function.":["u262"],"copy|Current-edition discussion, pp.":["u263"],"copy|214, 217.":["u264"],"copy|Frailty denotes vulnerability to incomplete restoration of homeostasis after a stressor.":["u363"],"copy|The Clinical Frailty Scale appears feasible and useful for 30-day mortality prediction, but prediction of returns/functional decline and benefit from acting on its result remain unestablished here.":["u364"],"copy|Table 15-4 compares Pro-Age, CFS, APOP, interRAI, ISAR, Rowland, Runciman, Silver Code, TRST and Variables Indicative of Placement; their outcomes and accuracy vary (p217).":["u365"],"copy|Prevent the next fall.":["u265"],"copy|Once the injury is treated, look at gait, vision, standing blood pressure, medicines, footwear and the home environment.":["u266"],"copy|A universal medication reduction strategy did not reduce falls at a year in the ED trial, while a different, brief individualized PT and pharmacy intervention reduced ED revisits at six months.":["u267"],"copy|The outcomes and follow-up intervals differ.":["u268"],"copy|ED fall prevention, pp.":["u269"],"copy|213–216.":["u270"],"copy|Fall prevention links emergency and inpatient care, PT/pharmacy, community programs, home modification and shared decision-making.":["u366"],"copy|Implementation must be assessed in real-world settings (Figure 15-6, p216).":["u367"],"copy|Who can act on the findings?":["u271"],"copy|Match a geriatric protocol to a person who can deliver it.":["u272"],"copy|EQUiPPED uses prescriber education, individual feedback and electronic decision support to improve medication safety.":["u273"],"copy|Observation permits reassessment of treatment response and time to obtain therapy, consultation or discharge support.":["u274"],"copy|These approaches can be delivered without building a separate geriatric ED.":["u275"],"copy|EQUiPPED, p.":["u276"],"copy|218 · Observation, p.":["u277"],"copy|218.":["u278"],"copy|Distinguish factors causing a fall from impaired protective responses and factors worsening injury.":["u368"],"copy|Examples: balance/vision/mobility deficits, sedating drugs and environmental hazards cause falls; poor reflexes, proprioception and core streng